## 1. Purpose & Scope
This Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) outlines the Gravimetric determination of Total Suspended Solids (TSS) and Total Dissolved Solids (TDS) in environmental water samples (e.g., surface water, groundwater, runoff). TSS represents the portion of solids retained by a glass fiber filter, while TDS represents the portion that passes through the filter and is dried to constant weight in a vessel.

## 2. Safety & Hazards
* **Thermal Hazards:** Use of drying ovens ($103^\circ\text{C} - 180^\circ\text{C}$) and muffle furnaces can cause severe thermal burns. Always utilize heat-resistant crucible tongs and thermal gloves when handling hot apparatus.
* **Ergonomic/Vacuum Hazards:** Vacuum filtration assemblies use low pressure. Ensure filtration flasks are borosilicate glass and free of visible cracks to eliminate implosion risks.
* **PPE:** Lab coat, safety glasses, and nitrile gloves.

## 3. Equipment & Materials
* Glass microfiber filter disks (e.g., Whatman 934-AH, $1.5\ \mu\text{m}$ pore size)
* Vacuum filtration apparatus (Buchner funnel, flask, and vacuum pump)
* Analytical balance capable of weighing to $\pm 0.1\text{ mg}$
* Drying oven regulated at $103^\circ\text{C}$ to $105^\circ\text{C}$ (TSS) and $180^\circ\text{C} \pm 2^\circ\text{C}$ (TDS)
* Desiccator with active color-indicating silica gel desiccant
* Evaporating dishes (porcelain or high-silica glass)

## 4. Operating Procedure

### 4.1 Filter and Dish Preparation
1. **TSS Filter Prep:** Place a glass fiber filter on the filtration apparatus. Apply vacuum and wash with three successive $20\text{ mL}$ volumes of deionized (DI) water. Continue vacuum until all trace water is removed. Transfer filter to an aluminum weigh pan, dry in an oven at $103^\circ\text{C} - 105^\circ\text{C}$ for 1 hour. Cool in a desiccator to room temperature and weigh to determine the tare mass ($B_{\text{TSS}}$).
2. **TDS Dish Prep:** Clean an evaporating dish and heat in an oven at $180^\circ\text{C} \pm 2^\circ\text{C}$ for 1 hour. Cool completely in a desiccator and record the stable tare mass ($B_{\text{TDS}}$).

### 4.2 Filtration & Sample Analysis
1. Assemble the pre-weighed filter pad into the filtration funnel and wet with a small volume of DI water to seal it against the porous plate. Turn on the vacuum.
2. Shake the sample bottle vigorously to achieve homogenous particle distribution. Quantitatively measure a known sample volume ($V$, typically $100\text{ mL}$ to $500\text{ mL}$ depending on turbidity) using a graduated cylinder.
3. Pour the measured sample into the filter funnel. Wash the cylinder and funnel walls with three successive $10\text{ mL}$ aliquots of DI water, allowing total drainage between washings.
4. **For TSS:** Carefully remove the filter pad from the funnel using blunt forceps. Place it back in its aluminum pan and dry in the oven at $103^\circ\text{C} - 105^\circ\text{C}$ for at least 1 hour. Cool in the desiccator and weigh to constant mass ($A_{\text{TSS}}$).
5. **For TDS:** Transfer the entire filtrate volume from the filter flask quantitatively into the pre-weighed evaporating dish. Place the dish into the drying oven at $180^\circ\text{C} \pm 2^\circ\text{C}$ until all liquid evaporates completely. Dry for at least an additional hour, cool inside the desiccator, and weigh to find the final mass ($A_{\text{TDS}}$).

## 5. Calculations
$$\text{TSS (mg/L)} = \frac{(A_{\text{TSS}} - B_{\text{TSS}}) \times 1,000,000}{V \text{ (mL)}}$$
$$\text{TDS (mg/L)} = \frac{(A_{\text{TDS}} - B_{\text{TDS}}) \times 1,000,000}{V \text{ (mL)}}$$
*Where:*
* $A = \text{Weight of dry filter/dish + residue (g)}$
* $B = \text{Tare weight of clean filter/dish (g)}$

